  "authority": "Library of Congress / American Library Association, Arabic Romanization Table (2012 version; earlier 2011, 1997)",
  "description": "Romanization used in US and many other library catalogues. Dots under emphatics (ṣ ḍ ṭ ẓ ḥ), digraphs th/kh/dh/sh/gh, ʻ (U+02BB) for ʿayn and ʼ (U+02BC) for hamza, á for alif maqṣūrah, article always al- with no sun-letter assimilation, ة = h (t in construct), ابن and بن both ibn.",
  "sources": [
    "https://www.loc.gov/catdir/cpso/romanization/arabic.pdf",
    "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Romanization_of_Arabic"
  ],
  "notes": [
    "Rules implemented from the LC table: initial hamza (also after the article) not written (8a); waṣlah not written, initial ٱ gives i (9); medial آ = ʼā (10b); final -iyy = ī, medial -iyy- = īy (6b-c, 11b); ـُوّ = ūw (11a); tanwīn ignored except the adverbial -an/-tan (12); al- never assimilated (17c); prime ʹ between letters that could be read as a digraph, Adʹham (21a); الله = Allāh (23); طه Ṭāhā, يس Yāsīn, عمرو ʻAmr (24); ابن/بن = ibn (25).",
    "Short vowels must be present in the input (or supplied by the name lexicon); unvocalised words are romanised letter by letter, with ي/و after a consonant read as ī/ū.",
    "Rule 18 lower-cases the article in all positions; the engine capitalises the first word of the string, so a name that starts with the article comes out as 'Al-…'.",
    "Names are romanized in pausal form: word-final case vowels are dropped and ة is always h/ah; the construct-state t (Wizārat al-…) needs syntactic knowledge and is not generated.",
    "Modern North African بن pronounced 'bin' (rule 25 exception) is not detected; it is always ibn."
  ],
  "alphabet": "ءآأؤإئابةتثجحخدذرزسشصضطظعغفقكلمنهوىيٱپچژگڤ",
